{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"absolute value absolute value","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md","fileName":"Absolute Value.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Absolute Value","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Number Systems and Arithmetic]] · [[Real Number Algebra and Arithmetic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","Real Number Algebra and Arithmetic","Number Line","Opposite Number","algebraessentials.pdf","foundationsofmath.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"absolute value absolute value","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md","fileName":"Absolute Value.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Absolute Value","heading":"Absolute Value","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The absolute value of a real number is its nonnegative distance from zero on the [[Number Line]]. It is defined by\n\n$$|a|=\\begin{cases}a,&a\\geq0,\\\\-a,&a<0.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThus (|5|=5) and (|-5|=5). Absolute value removes direction while preserving magnitude. The piecewise definition also explains why a variable's written sign is not enough to determine its value: (|x|) equals (x) only when (x) is nonnegative and equals its [[Opposite Number]] otherwise.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","Real Number Algebra and Arithmetic","Number Line","Opposite Number","algebraessentials.pdf","foundationsofmath.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Absolute Value.md","so